===Adverb===
{{la-adv|iam|-}}

# [[already]]
#: {{uxi|la|Birota '''iam''' refecta est.|The bicycle has '''already''' been repaired.}}
# [[now]]
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab urbe condita|6|42|2|quote=Refecti decumum iidem tribuni, deinde Sextius et Licinius dē decemvirīs sacrōrum ex parte dē plēbe creandīs lēgem pertulēre. creātī quīnque patrum quīnque plēbis; gradūque eō '''jam''' via facta ad consulātum vidēbātur.|t=Elected for the tenth time already, Sextus and Licinius the tribunes made a law for decemvirs of Rome's sacred rites to be issued in part from the people. Five were elected from the patricians, five others from the people; by this same move, a straight path to consulship had '''now''' been open to them.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|15|871|quote='''Iamque''' opus exegi, quod nec Iovis ira nec ignis nec poterit ferrum nec edax abolere vetustas.<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.met15.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans='''And now''', I have completed a great work, which not Jove's anger, and not fire nor steel, nor fast-consuming time can sweep away.<ref>[http://www.theoi.com/Text/OvidMetamorphoses15.html English]</ref>}}
# “iam iam,” repeated, doubly emphatic, e.g., [[now]] — [[now]], [[this]] [[very]] [[moment]], [[this]] [[instant]], [[any]] [[time]] [[now]], as of [[now]], [[now]] [[indeed]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Curculio|707|quote=CAPPADOX: '''Iam iam''' faciam ut iusseris.<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/plautus/curculio.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans=CAPPADOX: '''Now''', '''now''', I’ll do as you say (lit., as you will have ordered)!<ref>[http://artemis.austincollege.edu/acad/cml/rcape/comedy/5-3.html English]</ref>}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|4|371|thru=372|quote=“'''Iam iam''' nec maxuma Iūnō / nec Sāturnius haec oculīs pater aspicit aequīs.”|trans=“'''Right now''', neither do supreme Juno nor the Father [who is son of] Saturn regard these [matters] with the impartial eyes [of justice].”<br/>Or, “No '''longer''' do ….”}}
# [[anymore]]
# [[soon]]
# {{lb|la|in transitions}} [[now]], [[again]], [[moreover]], [[once]] [[more]]
#* {{Q|la|Titus Livius|Ab Urbe Condita|: 9.19|quote='''''Iam''' in opere quis par Romano miles?''|trans='''Moreover''', what soldier can match the Roman in entrenching?}}

====Usage notes====
{{m|la|iam|Iam}} means, generally, “at some point previous” or “since some point previous”. In English, {{m|en|already}}, the most common translation, is used only to emphasize that this point might have been expected to be later, whereas {{m|en|now}} is used to emphasize that the statement was once false, even when the statement refers to a point in the past or future. {{m|la|iam|Iam}} is used to express either. (Likewise, the most common Latin word for {{m|en|now}}, {{m|la|nunc}}, denotes only the literal present moment.) Also, where {{m|la|iam}} means {{m|en|now}}, it is often used in negative sentences, in which the most common English construction uses {{m|en|anymore}}.

However, note that when {{m|la|iam}} is strengthened as ''"iam iam"'' or ''"iam nunc"'', the meaning shifts to the present and has a meaning equivalent to {{m|la|nunc||now, at this exact moment}}.

